.TH ARCH 1 "2026-06-03" "Substrate OS" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
arch \- print machine hardware architecture
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B arch
.br
.B arch
[\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B arch
utility writes the machine hardware name to standard output, followed by a
newline.
It is equivalent to
.B uname \-m
and takes no operands.
.PP
On Substrate running the 32-bit x86 kernel the reported architecture is
.BR i386 .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display a usage message and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Output version information and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B arch
exits 0 on success, and >0 if the architecture could not be determined or an
unrecognized argument was given.
.SH EXAMPLE
Print the architecture:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ arch
i386
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR uname (1)
.SH STANDARDS
The
.B arch
utility is not part of POSIX.  Its behavior matches the GNU coreutils
.B arch
command, which prints the same string as
.BR "uname \-m" .
.SH HISTORY
An
.B arch
command appeared in 4.3BSD\-Reno and in GNU coreutils.
